To create a Mirth channel filter that drops duplicate messages based on the `MSH-10` (Message Control ID) segment, we'll use JavaScript as our scripting language.

**Filter Code:**
```javascript
// Set the filter name and description
var FILTER_NAME = "Drop Duplicates";
var FILTER_DESC = "Remove duplicates from incoming messages";

// Define a function to drop duplicate messages based on MSH-10 control ID
function dropDuplicates(message) {
  // Get the message's MSH-10 segment (Message Control ID)
  var msh10 = message.getSegment("MSH").getComponent(10).getValue();

  // Check if this is not a new message (i.e., it's already been processed)
  if (!msh10.startsWith("NEW")) {
    // If we reach this point, then we have a duplicate! Drop the message!
    return false;
  }

  // Otherwise, let's allow this message to pass through
  return true;
}

// Define our filter function
function filter(message) {
  try {
    // Call our `dropDuplicates` function above
    if (!dropDuplicates(message)) {
      // If we dropped the duplicate, then reject the original message!
      return false;
    }
  } catch (e) {
    // Log an error if we encounter any issues while processing this message
    log.error("Error processing message: " + e.getMessage());
  }

  // Otherwise, let's allow this message to pass through
  return true;
}

// Set our filter function as the default value for all incoming messages
filter = filter;

// Apply our filter function to each incoming message
var messages = getMessages();
for (var i = 0; i < messages.length(); i++) {
  var message = messages[i];
  if (!filter(message)) {
    // If we rejected this message, then drop it!
    log.info("Dropping duplicate: " + message.getSegment("MSH").getComponent(10).getValue());
  }
}
```
**How it works:**

1. We define a `dropDuplicates` function that takes an incoming message as input.
2. Inside the `dropDuplicates` function, we extract the MSH-10 segment (Message Control ID) from the original message using the `getSegment()` and `GetComponent()` methods.
3. We then check if this is not a new message (i.e., it's already been processed). If so, then we can be sure that this is an existing duplicate! In this case, let's drop the message by returning `false`.
4. Otherwise, let's allow this message to pass through and return `true`.

