1. We define two variables: `FILTER_NAME` and `FILTER_DESC`, which will be used to identify our filter.
2. The `dropDuplicates()` function takes an incoming message as input and checks if this is not a new message (i.e., no duplicates so far). If we reach this point, it means we have a duplicate! We drop the original message by rejecting it with `mirth.reject()`. Otherwise, let's allow this message to pass through.
3. Our filter logic is defined in the `filter()` function. We simply call the `dropDuplicates()` function and reject any messages that are not new (i.e., no duplicates so far).

