To create a Mirth channel filter that drops duplicate messages based on the `MSH-10` (Message Control ID) segment, we'll use JavaScript as our scripting language.

**Filter Code:**
```javascript
// Define the filter name and description
var FILTER_NAME = "Drop Duplicates";
var FILTER_DESC = "Remove duplicates from incoming messages";

// Set the filter's input message type to HL7v2 (default)
inputMessageTypes.push("HL7v2");

// Define a map to store unique MSH-10 values (control IDs)
var controlIdMap = new java.util.HashMap();

// Filter out duplicate messages based on Control ID
function processMessage(message) {
  // Get the incoming message's Control ID segment value
  var controlId = message.getSegment("MSH").getValue("10");

  // Check if this is a duplicate (i.e., already processed)
  if (!controlIdMap.containsKey(controlId)) {
    // If not, allow the message to pass through and store its Control ID in our map
    controlIdMap.put(controlId, true);
  } else {
    // If it's a duplicate, drop it!
    reject();
  }
}

// Define an error handler (in case of unexpected errors)
function onError(error) {
  log.error("Error processing message: " + error.getMessage());
}
```
**How the Filter Works:**

1. We define our filter name and description.
2. We set the input message type to HL7v2 (default).
3. We create a `java.util.HashMap` object, `controlIdMap`, to store unique MSH-10 values (Control IDs). This will help us identify duplicate messages.
4. In the `processMessage()` function, we extract the incoming message's Control ID segment value using the `getSegment()` and `getValue()` methods.
5. We then check if this is a duplicate by checking if our `controlIdMap` contains an entry with the same key (i.e., Control ID). If it does, that means we've already processed this message; in this case, we can drop it!
6. In case of unexpected errors or exceptions, we'll log an error using the `log.error()` method.

**Security Considerations:**

1. We only store unique MSH-10 values (Control IDs) in our `controlIdMap`, which is a minimal and secure approach.
2. We don't store any sensitive data, such as PHI (Protected Health Information).

