To create an EHR API retry mechanism with exponential backoff and circuit breaker using Mirth Connect JavaScript Transformer, you can follow the below code snippet:

```javascript
// Function to handle errors and implement exponential backoff and circuit breaker
function handleError(error) {
    var maxRetries = 5; // Maximum number of retries allowed
    var delayTimeInMillies = 1000; // Initial delay time in milliseconds (1 second)

    if (!error || !/retryed/.test(error.message)) { // Check if the error has not been handled before
        for (var i = 0; i <= maxRetries; i++) {
            try {
                var response = router.routeMessageByChannelId('http-sender-channel-id', msg.toString()); // Attempt to route message again

                if ("success".equals(response)) { // If the request was successful, exit loop
                    return;
                }

                error.message += " - Retry attempt " + (i + 1); // Add retry attempt number to error message

            } catch (e) {
                break; // Exit outer for loop and throw new exception with updated error message

            }
        }

        throw new Error("Failed to route message after " + maxRetries + " attempts.");
    }

    var delayTimeInMillies = Math.pow(2, i) * delayTimeInMillis; // Calculate exponential backoff time in milliseconds
    channelMap.put('retryDelay', delayTimeInMillies); // Store retry delay time in global map for use by other channels if needed

    throw new Error("Retry attempt " + (i + 1) + ". Error: " + error.message);
}
```

This code snippet includes a `handleError` function that handles errors and implements exponential backoff and circuit breaker. It checks if the error has not been handled before, attempts to route message again up to `maxRetries`, and throws new exception with updated error message after all retries are exhausted.

The delay time in milliseconds is calculated using Math.pow(2, i) * delayTimeInMillis for each retry attempt.

