To create a Mirth deployment script that exports channel configuration as an E4X-XML file, we'll follow these steps:

1. Get the `channelId` and `configFile` variables from the current thread
2. Create an empty XML file using the `<java.io.File>` class
3. Use the `org.dom.DocumentBuilderFactory.newDocumentBuilder().newDocument()` method to create a new E4X-XML document with the desired namespace (`http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom`)
4. Loop through each channel configuration element (e.g., `name`, `description`, etc.) and add it as an `<atom:entry>` element within our XML file using the `org.dom.DocumentBuilderFactory.newDocumentBuilder().newElement("atom:entry").setText()` method
5. Use a simple `try-catch` block to catch any potential errors that might occur during the export process

Here's the final code:
```javascript
// Get the channelId and configFile variables from the current thread
var channelId = getChannelId();
var configFile = getConfigFile();

// Create an empty XML file using the <java.io.File> class
var xmlFile = new java.io.File("/path/to/export/" + channelId.name() + ".xml");

try {
  // Create a new E4X-XML document with the desired namespace (http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom)
  var doc = org.dom.DocumentBuilderFactory.newDocumentBuilder().newDocument("http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom");

  // Loop through each channel configuration element and add it as an <atom:entry> element
  for each (var config in getChannelConfigurations(channelId)) {
    var entry = doc.createElement("atom:entry");
    entry.setText(config.name() + " - " + config.description());
    doc.appendChild(entry);
  }

  // Write the XML file to disk using a simple `java.io.FileWriter` object
  java.io.FileWriter writer = new java.io.FileWriter(xmlFile, true);
  writer.write(doc.toString());

} catch (e) {
  log.error("Error exporting channel configuration: " + e.getMessage());
}
```
