**Why this request?**

As healthcare organizations are required to protect the confidentiality of electronic protected health information (ePHI), it's essential to encrypt sensitive data at rest. This will ensure that even if an unauthorized party gains access to your database or storage device, they won't be able to read or modify sensitive patient data.

**Encryption wrapper using AES-256**

Here's a sample code example in Java using the popular OpenSSL library:
```java
import java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.spec.PSource;

public class PatientDataEncryptionWrapper {
    public static byte[] encryptPatientData(byte[] patientData, String encryptionKey) throws Exception {
        // Load the OpenSSL library
        PSource psource = new PSource(new ByteArrayInputStream(patientData));

        // Set up the AES-256 cipher with a 128-bit key (64 characters)
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/CBC/PKCS5");
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, encryptionKey.getBytes(), psource);

        // Encrypt the patient data using the AES-256 cipher
        ByteArrayOutputStream baos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        byte[] outputBuffer = new byte[1024];
        int written;
        while ((written = cipher.doOutput(outputBuffer)) != -1) {
            baos.write(outputBuffer, 0, written);
        }

        // Return the encrypted patient data as a byte array
        return baos.toByteArray();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Example usage: Encrypt some sample patient data (e.g. "John Smith" + SSN)
