To create a Mirth channel filter that drops duplicate messages based on the `MSH-10` (Message Control ID) segment, we'll use a combination of JavaScript and XML syntax.

**Filter Code:**
```xml
<filter name="Drop Duplicates">
  <script>
    // Get the message control ID from the MSH-10 segment
    var messageId = msg.get('MSH-10').getValue();

    // Check if this is not a duplicate (i.e., no other messages with the same ID have been processed)
    if (!channelMap.containsKey(messageId)) {
      // If we reach this point, it's a new message. Let's store its control ID in our map
      channelMap.put(messageId, messageId);
    } else {
      // This is a duplicate! Drop the message!
      return false;
    }
  </script>
</filter>
```
**How it works:**

1. We get the `MSH-10` segment's value using the `get()` method and store it in the `messageId` variable.
2. We check if this is not a duplicate (i.e., no other messages with the same ID have been processed) by checking if our channel map contains an entry with the same key (`channelMap.containsKey()`). If we reach this point, it means we've already seen this message control ID before, so we can drop it!
3. If this is not a duplicate (i.e., no other messages with the same ID have been processed), then it's a new message! Let's store its control ID in our map using `channelMap.put()`.

