As a healthcare integration expert specializing in mirth, I'll provide you with a comprehensive solution to validate an HL7 v2 message against the requirements outlined by reference documentation.

**HL7v2 Message Validator (Java)**

Create a new Java project and add the following code:
```java
import org.hl7.v3.MSH;
import org.hl7.v3.Segment;

public class Hl7v2MessageValidator {
    public static void validate(String hl7Message) throws Exception {
        // Parse HL7 message into an MSH object
        MSH msh = new MSH(hl7Message);

        // Validate required segments and field lengths
        if (!validateSegments(msh)) throw new Exception("Invalid segment(s)");
        if (!validateFieldLengths(msh)) throw new Exception("Invalid field length(s)");

    }

    private boolean validateSegments(MSH msh) {
        // Check for required segments (MSH, PID, PV1)
        Segment[] segments = msh.getSegments();
        if (!Arrays.asList(new String[]{"MSH", "PID", "PV1"}).containsAll(segments.map(Segment::getName))) return true;

        // Validate segment lengths
        for (Segment segment : segments) {
            int length = segment.getLength();
            if ((segment.getName().equals("MSH") || segment.getName().equals("PID")) && !isValidLength(length, 20)) throw new Exception("Invalid MSH/PID length");
            if ((segment.getName().equals("PV1") || segment.getName().equals("OBX")) && !isValidLength(length, 50)) throw new Exception("Invalid PV1/OBX length");
        }
    }

    private boolean validateFieldLengths(MSH msh) {
        // Check for required field lengths
        if (!isValidLength(msh.getMSH_3(), 20)) throw new Exception("Invalid MSH-3 (Sending Application) length");

    }

    private int isValidLength(int length, int maxLen) {
        return (length > maxLen) ? "Invalid" : true;
    }
}
```
