To create a Mirth deployment script that exports channel configuration as an E4X XML file, we'll follow these steps:

1. Get the `channelId` and `exportFile` variables from the current thread
2. Create an empty XML file using the `java.io.File` class
3. Use the `E4X XML Builder` library to create a new `<channel>` element with the desired attributes (e.g., `id`, `name`, etc.)
4. Loop through each child node (`<rec`) of the channel and add its contents as an E4X XML attribute (`attributeName="rec/" + rec.childName().toString() + ">"`)
5. Use a `try-catch` block to catch any exceptions that might occur while exporting the data

Here's the final code:
```javascript
// Get the channelId and exportFile variables from the current thread
var channelId = channel.id;
var exportFile = new java.io.File("/path/to/export/" + channelId.name() + ".xml");

try {
  // Create an empty XML file using the java.io.File class
  var xmlFile = new java.io.FileWriter(exportFile);
  xmlFile.write("<channel xmlns='http://www.mirth.org'><name>" + channelId.name() + "</name></channel>");

  // Use E4X XML Builder to create a new <channel> element with desired attributes
  var channelXml = e4x.xmlBuilder().createXML("<channel xmlns='http://www.mirth.org'>");
  channelXml.addAttribute("id", channelId.id());
  channelXml.addAttribute("name", channelId.name());

  // Loop through each child node (`<rec`) of the channel and add its contents as an E4X XML attribute
  for (var rec in channelId.children()) {
    var attributeName = "rec/" + rec.childName().toString() + ">";
    channelXml.addAttribute(attributeName, rec.toString());
  }

  // Write the exported data to the file using a `try-catch` block
  try {
    xmlFile.write(channelXml.toString());
} catch (java.io.IOException e) {
  log.error("Error writing XML file: " + e.getMessage());

// Close the file writer
xmlFile.close();

// Log any errors that might have occurred during export
if (!log.isError()) log.info("Export successful!");
```
