The issue you're facing is called a "half-open" scenario! When a client (in this case, your Mirth channel) sends an HL7 message and doesn't receive any response from the server (in this case, the EHR system), it will keep trying to send more messages until it runs out of retries or hits its maximum number of attempts.

To fix this issue, let's add a simple retry mechanism with exponential backoff. We'll use Java code to achieve this:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public void sendHL7Message(String hl7Message) {
    int maxRetries = 5; // adjust your desired value
    AtomicInteger retries = new AtomicInteger(0);

    while (retries.get() < maxRetries) {
        try {
            // attempt to send the HL7 message
            socket.send(hl7Message.getBytes());
            break;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            if (!e.getMessage().startsWith("EHR system")) { // adjust your desired error filter
                System.out.println("Error sending HL7 message: " + e.getMessage());
            }

            // exponential backoff and retry
            int delayMs = 1000 * Math.pow(2, retries.get()); // adjust your desired value (in this case, 1 second)
            TimeUtil.sleep(delayMs);
        }
    }
}
```

