To create a Mirth deployment script that exports the channel configuration as an E4X XML file, we'll use the following code:
```
// Set the output file name and path
var fileName = "channel-config-" + new Date().getTime() + ".xml";
var filePath = "/path/to/output/" + fileName;

try {
  // Get the channel config object
  var channelConfig = router.getChannel("my-channel");

  // Create an E4X XML writer to write the output file
  var xmlWriter = java.io.FileWriter(filePath);
  var e4xXmlWriter = org.apache.axis2.AxisException.createXMLSerializer();
  e4xXmlWriter.setIndent(32);

  // Loop through each segment in the channel config and export it as an E4X XML node
  for (var seg in channelConfig.getSegments()) {
    var segNode = e4xXmlWriter.createElement(seg.getName());
    segNode.setText(seg.toString());

    // Add any child nodes to the segment node
    for (var childSeg in seg.getChildSegments()) {
      segNode.addChild(childSeg.getName(), childSeg.toString());
    }

    // Write the segment node as an E4X XML element
    e4xXmlWriter.writeStartElement(segNode.getQname().getLocalName());
    e4xXmlWriter.writeNamespacePrefixes();
    e4xXmlWriter.writeAttributes();

    // Add any namespace declarations to the segment node
    for (var ns in seg.getName().getNamespaceNamespaces()) {
      e4xXmlWriter.writeNamespaceDeclaration(ns.getNamespaceURI(), ns.getLocalName());
    }
  }

  // Close the E4X XML writer
  xmlWriter.close();
} catch (e) {
  // Log any errors that occur during execution
  log.error("Error exporting channel config: " + e.getMessage());
}
```
This script uses the Mirth JavaScript API to access and iterate through each segment in the channel configuration. It then creates an E4X XML writer to write the output file, loops through each segment and exports it as an E4X XML node using the `e4xXmlWriter` object, and writes each segment node as an E4X XML element.

